 ('p', 'The key is restraint and context. A couple of well-chosen emoji clarify and warm a message; a dozen scattered through it become noise. And register matters, playful in a chat with friends, sparing or none in a formal document. Matching emoji use to the setting is what separates effective use from clutter.'),
 ('tool', 'emoji-picker', 'Emoji Picker', 'Search, browse and copy any emoji to your clipboard, entirely in your browser.'),
 ('h2', 'Emoji among the character tools'),
 ('p', 'Because emoji are characters, they connect to the other tools that deal with text at the code level. A <a href="../tools/text-to-ascii.html">text to ASCII</a> tool reveals the numeric codes behind characters, which is exactly what emoji are under the surface; a <a href="../tools/character-counter.html">character counter</a> shows why an emoji-laden message can hit a length limit faster than expected; and a <a href="../tools/meme-generator.html">meme generator</a> puts emoji and text together for visual jokes. Seeing emoji as part of the text system, rather than as separate images, ties all of this together.'),
 ('h2', 'Emoji, accessibility and search'),
 ('p', "Two practical details are worth knowing because they affect how emoji are received beyond how they look. The first is accessibility: screen readers, which read text aloud for people with visual impairments, announce emoji by their official Unicode names. A 'face with tears of joy' is read out as exactly that. This means a message packed with emoji can become a tiring stream of spoken descriptions for someone using a screen reader, and an emoji used in place of a word forces the listener to infer meaning from its name. Using emoji in moderation, and not relying on them to carry essential meaning, keeps your writing accessible to everyone."),
 ('p', 'The second is that emoji carry meaning that can shift with context and over time, sometimes in ways the sender does not intend. Because they are a shared visual vocabulary, particular emoji acquire connotations, some playful, some ironic, some that differ sharply between generations or communities, and an emoji meant innocently can read very differently to another audience. The safest approach in anything that matters is to use emoji whose common meaning is clear and uncontroversial, and to lean on them for tone and emphasis rather than as coded messages open to misreading. Combined with the earlier point that the recipient may see different artwork than you sent, this is a reminder that emoji, for all their friendliness, are a form of communication that rewards a little care. Understanding that they are shared characters with device-specific looks and culturally-shaped meanings, not private pictures, is what lets you use them to add genuine warmth and clarity rather than accidental confusion, and a good picker simply makes finding the right one quick so you can focus on using it well.'),
 ('p', 'To recap: emoji are Unicode characters, not pictures, which is why they behave like text, countable, copyable, searchable, and why each device draws its own version, so the same emoji can look different to your recipient. Some are built from several combined codes, hence the odd character counts, and new ones arrive with each Unicode update. Used with restraint and an eye to context, emoji add tone, clarity and warmth that plain text lacks. Understand that they are characters at heart, pick them from a good picker, and use a few well rather than many carelessly, and emoji become a genuinely useful part of how you write.'),
